References: <CAKH7RFDov11fFaQovGOM8smMdKb=ptCgxnXE8z22pGoRKCZwzQ@mail.gmail.com>	<52710674.8020700@fhcrc.org>
	<CAKH7RFDsRYp0vV5RjeJ-Atqgm8kdh=6pVNSdTottNumqwOgfzQ@mail.gmail.com>
Message-ID: <52715420.6060906@fhcrc.org>

If these are viewed in a report, e.g.,

   example(qa)
   browseURL(report(qa))

the figure is preceeded by text: "The reddish lines are quartiles (solid: 
median, dotted: 25, 75), the green line is the mean. Shading is proporitional to 
number of reads." The shading can be confusing; try to think of the figure 
initially with just the lines.

The green line is easiest to understand -- the mean quality. Usually it is high 
across all cycles and dropping in the final few; your initial bases have 
variable quality, which is unusual. In particular at cycle ten more than 25% 
(lower dotted red lines) of reads had quality <5, the distribution is mostly 
bimodal, as indicated by the dark shading at the bottom and top of the region 
above cycle 10. Cycles 11, 12, and 13 show mean quality recovering; the 25% 
quartile and shaded areas at quality scores 17-19 at cycle 11, 27 and 31 at 
cycle 12, and 35 and 37 at cycle 13 suggest that there are many reads with 
similar qualities. The continuous grey area throughout the figure after cycle 10 
indicates that at each cycle a non-trivial fraction of reads had low quality; it 
is more usual for many cycles to appear white.

